  "preamble": "The diphthong [au] monophthongizes to open-mid [ɔ], the two elements fusing into a single rounded back vowel. Latin au became open /ɔ/ in Italian (aurum > oro /ˈɔ.ro/, causa > cosa /ˈkɔ.za/), distinct from the close /o/ of Latin ō and ŭ. In English the Middle English diphthong /au/ of law and claw had become an open back monophthong by 1700, giving Received Pronunciation /ɔː/.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Monophthongization",
  "languageExamples": [
